# Netlify redirects. One rule per line: from  to  status
# https://docs.netlify.com/manage/routing/redirects/redirect-options/

# g6.html -> whitespace-as-ui-component (slug renamed for SEO)
/guide/g6.html   /guide/whitespace-as-ui-component   301

# NOTE on hiding .html: the reliable way to do this on Netlify is the
# built-in "Pretty URLs" toggle (Site settings > Build & deploy >
# Post processing > Asset optimization), not a custom rule here.
# Netlify's own placeholder syntax doesn't cleanly support a rule like
# "/guide/:slug.html -> /guide/:slug" (a placeholder glued to a literal
# suffix in the same segment isn't part of its documented pattern
# matching), and by default Netlify won't run a rule against a URL that
# already matches a real file on disk (the .html files) unless the rule
# is force-flagged -- so a hand-rolled version of this is easy to get
# subtly wrong. Pretty URLs is Netlify's own tested implementation of
# exactly this behavior, so it doesn't have that risk.

# /favicon.ico -> the site icon. Browsers and crawlers ask for this path
# by default when they have no <link rel="icon"> to read (a directly
# opened image or XML URL, some link previewers). There is no .ico file,
# so it used to fall through to the HTML 404 page. Status 200 makes this
# a rewrite, not a redirect: the PNG is served at /favicon.ico itself.
/favicon.ico    /assets/favicon.png    200

# ── Colour Tokens: removed (Phase 4) ─────────────────────────────
# The Colour Tokens feature was deleted. Every URL it served returns
# 410 Gone. NONE of them redirects to /palettes.
#
# WHY 410 AND NOT A REDIRECT
# The content is gone, not moved. /palettes is a different resource, so
# a 301 there would be a soft-404: search engines treat a redirect to an
# irrelevant page as a 404 anyway, and it would strand anyone following
# an old link on a page that does not answer their request. 410 says the
# URL is intentionally and permanently gone, which de-indexes it faster
# and more cleanly than a 404.
#
# SYNTAX: "from  to  status". Netlify requires the `to` field to be a
# real path even for an error status, so it is "/" throughout; the status
# column is what is served. ("404!  410" is NOT valid — the `!` force
# suffix belongs on a status code, not on a destination.)
#
# Listed explicitly rather than as /tokens/* so the set of deleted URLs
# is auditable one-for-one against the removed sitemap entries and the
# ALLOWED_REMOVED list in scripts/qa/check-urls.js, and so a future
# /tokens-something path cannot be swallowed by accident. Netlify's
# parser supports no regex and no mid-path wildcards in any case.
#
# NOT VERIFIABLE LOCALLY: whether the edge really returns 410 can only be
# confirmed against a deployment. Netlify has served 410 from _redirects
# as documented, but there is at least one open report of it being
# downgraded to 404. Re-check after deploy; if they come back 404 the
# rules are still correct as written and the fallback is an edge
# function. Either way they must never become a redirect to /palettes.

